1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
What may you keep about a card?

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:08,208
In Lesson 1, the buyer typed the card straight into the card zone, and ecom got a token instead of the number.

3
00:00:08,258 --> 00:00:14,544
In Lesson 2, that card was all the buyer's bank needed to say yes, so whoever holds a card number can pay with it.

4
00:00:14,594 --> 00:00:19,992
This lesson names what each part of the system may keep about a card, and what it never keeps.

5
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:25,032
When your bank calls you, they say: your card ending seven eight nine zero.

6
00:00:25,082 --> 00:00:27,264
They never read out the whole number.

7
00:00:27,314 --> 00:00:29,952
And they never ask for the three digits on the back.

8
00:00:30,002 --> 00:00:32,232
Now picture a support engineer at a shop.

9
00:00:32,282 --> 00:00:39,168
A payment failed, so the engineer pastes the buyer's whole card number into a support ticket, to ask the bank about it.

10
00:00:39,218 --> 00:00:41,256
The number now lives in the ticket.

11
00:00:41,306 --> 00:00:46,632
It lives in the ticket's backups, in its search, and with everyone who can read tickets.

12
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:51,768
One paste has copied a card number into many places, and none of them was built to guard it.

13
00:00:51,818 --> 00:00:55,128
Systems should talk about cards the way your bank does on the phone.

14
00:00:55,178 --> 00:00:57,000
Here are the words for that.

15
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:01,320
The long number on the front of the card is the PAN, the card's own number.

16
00:01:01,370 --> 00:01:04,080
It is the one piece a thief needs most.

17
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:10,248
In this system it lives in one place only: the card zone, the locked room from the first lesson.

18
00:01:10,298 --> 00:01:13,152
The three digits on the back are the card's security code.

19
00:01:13,202 --> 00:01:17,904
The card industry's rules say nobody may keep it once a payment is approved.

20
00:01:17,954 --> 00:01:21,408
This system goes further: it never even asks for it.

21
00:01:21,458 --> 00:01:25,656
When screens and reports need to show a card, they show the masked number.

22
00:01:25,706 --> 00:01:29,904
That is the first six digits and the last four, with stars between.

23
00:01:29,954 --> 00:01:33,528
The first six digits only tell which bank gave out the card.

24
00:01:33,578 --> 00:01:36,504
The last four let a person recognise their own card.

25
00:01:36,554 --> 00:01:40,680
Nothing in between is shown, so the masked number cannot be used to pay.

26
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:45,984
And when another system needs to point at a card, to charge it later, it keeps a card token.

27
00:01:46,034 --> 00:01:49,680
A card token is a random name the card zone gives the card.

28
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:51,552
Nothing in it comes from the number.

29
00:01:51,602 --> 00:01:54,216
Make two tokens, and they share nothing.

30
00:01:54,266 --> 00:01:57,312
So a token tells a thief nothing about the card.

31
00:01:57,362 --> 00:01:59,736
Three words, three rules.

32
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:02,112
The PAN lives only in the card zone.

33
00:02:02,162 --> 00:02:03,984
The masked number may be shown.

34
00:02:04,034 --> 00:02:05,856
The token may be kept.

35
00:02:05,906 --> 00:02:08,784
And the code on the back is never kept by anyone.

36
00:02:08,834 --> 00:02:10,656
Now watch the product.

37
00:02:10,706 --> 00:02:13,080
A buyer opens the bank's payment page.

38
00:02:13,130 --> 00:02:15,528
Under "Card details" sits the card form.

39
00:02:15,578 --> 00:02:17,832
It asks for the number and the date on the card.

40
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:19,992
There is no box for the code on the back.

41
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:22,560
The buyer types the card and continues.

42
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:29,784
The page now shows the card as one short line: the masked number, the name of the buyer's bank, and its card network.

43
00:02:29,834 --> 00:02:31,848
The full number is gone from the screen.

44
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:35,016
Now open the bank admin portal and its Payments page.

45
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:36,888
Each payment has a Card column.

46
00:02:36,938 --> 00:02:42,216
Every row shows a masked number: six digits, stars, four digits.

47
00:02:42,266 --> 00:02:45,024
Nowhere in the portal is there a full card number.

48
00:02:45,074 --> 00:02:49,368
The bank's own staff see exactly what your bank says on the phone, and no more.

49
00:02:49,418 --> 00:02:51,240
What does this protect?

50
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:54,600
A masked number on a screen leaks nothing useful.

51
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:59,592
A token in a database leaks nothing either, because it is only a random name.

52
00:02:59,642 --> 00:03:04,128
The PAN, if it leaks, lets a thief try to spend the buyer's money.

53
00:03:04,178 --> 00:03:09,672
So the PAN stays in one room, and everything else gets a smaller, safer word.

54
00:03:09,722 --> 00:03:14,640
This protects the buyer first, and then the shop and the bank, who would pay for the leak.

55
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:16,512
Back to the support ticket.

56
00:03:16,562 --> 00:03:20,952
It should have held the masked number, or the token, and never the PAN.

57
00:03:21,002 --> 00:03:25,536
With either one, the bank can still find the payment, and the ticket leaks nothing.

58
00:03:25,586 --> 00:03:33,120
Your bank never asks for the code on the back, and our systems never hold the PAN: they hold the masked number and the token.

59
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:35,568
Next: where a card number may live.
